WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.400
Your tattoos are inside your immune system,&nbsp;
literally. With each very tasteful piece of art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.720
you kick start a drama with millions of deaths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:16.880
grand sacrifices and your immune system&nbsp;
stepping in to protect you from yourself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.207
Let's give you a tattoo and zoom in&nbsp;
to see what happens inside your skin.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.070
The Conveyor Belt of Death

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.120
Your skin has to solve a huge problem – it's your&nbsp;
largest organ and has the most direct contact with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:38.027
the world around you. Trillions of microbes, dirt,&nbsp;
insects and vermin can’t be allowed to get inside you

00:00:38.027 --> 00:00:43.240
– but your skin is also constantly&nbsp;
damaged by you moving through the world.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:49.080
Your body solved this by making your skin a&nbsp;
conveyor belt of death. All the skin you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:55.240
is actually dead stuff. The alive part of your&nbsp;
skin cells begins around one millimeter deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:01.160
in the skin industrial complex. Stem cells&nbsp;
constantly clone themselves producing new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:05.000
skin cells that begin a journey&nbsp;
from the inside to the outside.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.200
Each new generation pushes the older ones&nbsp;
further up. As your skin cells mature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.080
they interlock with each other and produce&nbsp;
Lamellar bodies, tiny bags that squirt out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.546
fat to create a waterproof coat that&nbsp;
closes any gaps between them. 

00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:24.920
And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;they dry out and kill themselves,&nbsp;
merging together into inseparable lumps.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:30.640
This wall of dead corpses is consistently&nbsp;
pushed upwards. Up to 50 layers of dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.960
cells cover your whole body and are constantly&nbsp;
replaced by new cells moving up. Every hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:42.400
you shed around 200,000,000 dead skin cells and&nbsp;
all the dirt or bacteria that are stuck to them.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.640
Tattooing this part of your skin would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.681
useless as nothing would stick&nbsp;
around. We need to go deeper.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.680
When the Fleshy World Explodes

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.360
Below the conveyor belt of death lies the&nbsp;
dermis. It's full of structural tissue and cells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.000
tiny blood vessels, sensory cells that report&nbsp;
to nerve endings, the roots of your hairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.920
sweat glands regulating your temperature.&nbsp;
And of course loads of immune cells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:10.760
guarding your flesh right&nbsp;
below the moving border wall.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:16.800
This region and below is where&nbsp;
your new tattoo will go. Ok! Ready?

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.960
The world explodes. Half a dozen monoliths&nbsp;
the size of skyscrapers slam through the fifty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.600
layers of dead cells, deep into the dermis,&nbsp;
ripping huge holes into the skin – only to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:33.440
retreat and smash through the tissue again&nbsp;
about twice a second. Tens of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:39.240
of cells are violently killed right away,&nbsp;
ripped into pieces or damaged beyond repair.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:43.840
Luckily, you did your research and chose&nbsp;
a responsible tattoo artist who properly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:49.560
disinfected their tools and your skin. But&nbsp;
you only ever get 99.9% of all bacteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.040
and some of the survivors made it into your flesh.&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.400
To put it mildly, your immune system is not&nbsp;
amused at all! All the death and destruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.160
wakes up hundreds of thousands of Macrophages in&nbsp;
your dermis, that rush into the open wounds to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.960
defend you. Immediately they start killing&nbsp;
bacteria, release chemicals that call for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:13.000
reinforcements and order your blood vessels to&nbsp;
open up and make your dermis swell up with fluid.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.680
But worse than the hundreds of wounds&nbsp;
and a few invaders is the tidal wave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:21.760
of chemicals that floods your tissue. Tattoo&nbsp;
ink can be made from hundreds of substances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.800
some may even be toxic or carcinogenic.&nbsp;
Most are from heavy metals like lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.160
nickel or chromium dissolved in distilled water.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.240
The battlefield is now a wild mix of&nbsp;
dead cell parts, a few panicked bacteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:39.120
blood and bodily fluids, platelet&nbsp;
cells trying to close wounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.040
more and more fresh immune cells&nbsp;
and the flood of tattoo ink.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.080
On the scale of your cells, clumps of&nbsp;
ink particles are huge – if you were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.040
the size of a cell, they’d range from&nbsp;
big dogs to small office buildings.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:04:00.086
Your immune system has one main job: Identify&nbsp;
what is not you and smash it until it's dead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.086 --> 00:04:04.360
The Macrophages are desperately&nbsp;
trying to do that. Like tiny octopuses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.240
they extend arm-like structures and begin&nbsp;
pulling the ink particles inside. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:14.880
when a Macrophage has eaten an enemy, it showers&nbsp;
it in acid to dissolve it. But this doesn’t work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:21.480
with the ink. They try and try but nothing&nbsp;
works, the particles don't react in any way.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.875
And this is just the particles&nbsp;
small enough to be devoured.

00:04:24.875 --> 00:04:29.240
By now the larger chunks are surrounded 
by&nbsp;thousands of your structural skin cells

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:33.840
and macrophages that are nomming on them,&nbsp;
bathing them in acid and attack chemicals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.640
trying to destroy and kill them. But&nbsp;
they are not moving even a tiny bit.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.400
Nothing works!

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.680
Finally your immune system has to concede. It&nbsp;
will not win this fight – so it does the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.960
best thing: Not lose. Your cells don’t know&nbsp;
how dangerous these metals and chemicals are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.880
but they can at least not let them spread&nbsp;
around. So they just stay in place. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:00.000
vacuum up all the particles they can fit&nbsp;
into their bodies and surround the larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.080
ones trapping them in the only prison they can&nbsp;
build: themselves. Bit by bit, the ink inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.560
thousands of tiny wounds moves inside millions&nbsp;
of immune cells that freeze in place forever.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.960
On the outside you don’t notice any of&nbsp;
this. Your new tattoo is fresh and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:21.480
colours vibrant. Your skin hurts and is&nbsp;
irritated and swollen. But wounds heal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:27.720
tiny holes close, dead cells are replaced. Bit&nbsp;
by bit, the conveyor belt of death does its job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.000
shedding dead cells ripe in colour, replacing&nbsp;
them with fresh and clean ones. Your tattoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:38.120
becomes a little less vibrant, now the ink&nbsp;
is no longer on your skin but inside it.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.080
But what you are really seeing&nbsp;
is millions of your Macrophages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:45.080
sitting in your dermis, patiently holding&nbsp;
the ink in place, protecting your body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:50.480
from poison. Your immune system&nbsp;
is why your tattoo is forever.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.160
Actually Nothing is Forever

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:57.480
Over time your Macrophages get old and die&nbsp;
and new ones come in to gobble up the ink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:02.200
and keep it in place. But sometimes&nbsp;
a tiny bit of ink escapes. Most of it

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:07.560
is recaptured and locked in place,&nbsp;
but not always the exact same place.

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:13.160
You notice that as your tattoo fades out a bit&nbsp;
and turns less sharp and crisp at its edges.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:18.480
Some of the ink escapes the tattoo entirely.&nbsp;
It rides fluids flowing from your tissue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:24.600
and spreads around your body, another reason&nbsp;
why tattoo ink should ideally not be poison.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:29.400
Your immune system also kind of doesn’t want&nbsp;
you to remove tattoos – to do that usually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:34.120
ink is shot at with lasers, which heats up the&nbsp;
particles until they break into smaller chunks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:38.000
cooking your brave Macrophages in the&nbsp;
process. With every round of lasering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.893
more of your tattoo is broken down and&nbsp;
carried away by fluids. But also every time

00:06:42.893 --> 00:06:48.320
new Macrophages rush into the tattoo&nbsp;
to lock the ink in place. So like uhm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.200
maybe think about it carefully before you get the&nbsp;
name of your new bae tattooed, but you do you.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:59.040
But if you got one, you can directly see&nbsp;
your immune system protecting you. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.760
is how much your body loves you, which&nbsp;
is kind of sweet. And while tattoos are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.680
probably not that big of a deal for your&nbsp;
body if applied correctly: you now know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:13.120
about the struggle going on inside your skin&nbsp;
and the sacrifice of your Macrophage buddies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.949
only for you to have that art forever.

